  "query_preview": "..Still shaping up to be a bit more of an active \nday for storms today across southeast Arizona with the main \nconcerns being strong to severe outflow winds resulting in \nblowing dust. Isolated instances of heavy rainfall leading to \nflash flooding cannot be ruled out. Best storm chances will be \nacross eastern Pima County eastward. Timing of storms across the\nTucson Metro look to be the start of the evening commute \nthrough about 7-8 pm MST. People traveling during this period\nshould remain alert for possible sudden visibility changes and \navoid driving through flooded roads.\n\nThe pattern supporting the uptick in storms is a bit more nuanced \nsetup for the Monsoon. The subtropical high is centered to our south \nnear the Baja Spur putting southeast Arizona on the fringe of the \nupper circulation with a weak upper low nudging into Arizona from \nCalifornia. Between these features, the air will tend to spread out \nand allow for rising motion supporting the potential for taller \nstorms. Without the high centered directly overhead, we see \ntemperatures only slightly above normal for this time of year. We \nalso have sufficient mid-level moisture in place for storms with \nGOES West Total Precipitable Water product estimating values of \naround 0.8 to 1.3 inches. With these numbers in mind and knowing we \nare anticipating a bit of drier air intruding into the mid/upper \nlevels we will see a threat for both heavy rainfall and strong \noutflow wind this afternoon and evening.\n\nThere are multiple avenues that would support convective initiation \nthis afternoon including both surface and terrain convergence. High \nresolution guidance depicts storms first firing across higher \nterrain areas in the White and Chiricahua Mountains around 1-2 PM. \nThereafter, storms start to develop in and around the Tucson Metro \nboth from surface and terrain convergence. Guidance also suggests \nthat outflows driven by these storms could be quite strong and \ntrigger additional storms persisting into the evening hours. Dry air \nentrainment in the mid-levels of the storms could enhance their \ndowndrafts with DCAPE values in the 1500-2000 J/kg range suggestive \nof a few gusts approaching or exceeding severe criteria (58 mph). \nWinds this strong can cause damage to structures and loft widespread \nareas of blowing dust. Therefore, we coordinated with the Storm \nPrediction Center (SPC) to add southeast Arizona into a Marginal \nRisk (level 1 of 5) for severe thunderstorms producing damaging \nwinds. There is also a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for isolated \ninstances of flash flooding as locally heavy rainfall rates cannot \nbe ruled out with any slower moving storms.\n\nThe extended forecast continues to be on track as well with another \nday of storms tomorrow but with fewer of the ingredients lining up \nand for much less of the area. However, isolated showers and storms \nwill form over the White Mountains with the potential for gusty \nwinds again as the upper low continues to nudge east. By Friday, the \nupper low will phase in with the Western US longwave trough and \neject northward. The result will be drier weather with broad \nwesterly flow. Look for temperatures to remain within degrees of \nnormal through the weekend, then trend warmer as high pressure \nbuilds into the region next week.",
